    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find \\(\\sin F\\) in right triangle \\(DEF\\), we need to determine the angles \\(D\\), \\(E\\), and \\(F\\) and their corresponding sines. Let's start by understanding the given information and the relationships in a right triangle.\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin E = 1\n\\]\n\nIn a right triangle, the sum of the non-right angles must be \\(90^\\circ\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\nD + E + F = 90^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin E = 1\\), angle \\(E\\) must be \\(90^\\circ\\) because \\(\\sin 90^\\circ = 1\\). Thus, angle \\(E\\) is the right angle in the right triangle \\(DEF\\).\n\nWith angle \\(E\\) being the right angle, the remaining angles \\(D\\) and \\(F\\) must sum to:\n\\[\nD + F = 90^\\circ\n\\]\n\nGiven \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\), we can use the Pythagorean identity to find \\(\\cos D\\):\n\\[\n\\sin^2 D + \\cos^2 D = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)^2 + \\cos^2 D = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{25}{169} + \\cos^2 D = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = 1 - \\frac{25}{169}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = \\frac{169}{169} - \\frac{25}{169}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = \\frac{144}{169}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos D = \\frac{12}{13} \\quad (\\text{since } \\cos D \\text{ is positive in the first quadrant})\n\\]\n\nNow, we know that in right triangle \\(DEF\\):\n\\[\n\\cos D = \\sin F\n\\]\nThus:\n\\[\n\\sin F = \\frac{12}{13}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\sin F\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{12}{13}}\n\\]",
